% Pixel_webp(n) @PACKAGE_VERSION@ | WebP encoding and decoding for Pixel
% Cyan Ogilvie
% @PACKAGE_VERSION@


# NAME

Pixel\_webp - Encode pmaps as WebP images and decode WebP images to pmaps


# SYNOPSIS

**package require Pixel_webp** ?@PACKAGE_VERSION@?

**pixel::webp::encode** ?*-option value* ...? *pmap* *quality*

**pixel::webp::decode** ?*-option value* ...? *webp_data*

**pixel::webp::info** *webp_data*


# DESCRIPTION

This package converts between Pixel's pmaps (32-bit BGRA images) and WebP
images, using libwebp.  The encoder exposes the libwebp configuration
(**WebPConfig**) as options, so that callers can trade size, speed and quality,
control how the alpha channel is stored, and see what the encoder did.


# COMMANDS

**pixel::webp::encode** ?*-option value* ...? *pmap* *quality*
:   Encode *pmap* and return the WebP image as a byte array.  *quality* is the
    lossy quality, from 0 (smallest) to 100 (best), and may be fractional.
    With **-lossless** it is instead the effort spent compressing, from 0
    (fastest) to 100 (smallest).

    With no options the result is the same as libwebp's simple
    **WebPEncodeBGRA** API gives: a lossy image, encoded with method 4, with
    the alpha channel (if any) stored losslessly.  The alpha channel is only
    written when some pixel of *pmap* isn't fully opaque: an opaque *pmap*
    gives a plain lossy WebP with no alpha.

    The options, which must come before *pmap*, are:

    **-preset** *preset*
    :   The **WebPConfigPreset** the configuration starts from before the other
        options are applied: **default** (the default), **picture**, **photo**,
        **drawing**, **icon** or **text**.

    **-method** *method*
    :   The speed and size trade-off, from 0 (fastest, largest) to 6 (slowest,
        smallest).  The default is 4.  It also sets the effort spent on a
        lossless alpha channel: with **-alpha_quality 100**, method 6 can be
        many times slower than method 5 on images with complex transparency.

    **-lossless**
    :   Encode losslessly (a VP8L image).  *quality* is then the compression
        effort.

    **-near_lossless** *level*
    :   With **-lossless**, preprocess the image to compress better while
        changing pixels by up to an amount set by *level*: 0 changes them the
        most, 100 (the default) not at all.  It's an error without
        **-lossless**.

    **-alpha** *mode*
    :   How the alpha channel of *pmap* is used: **auto** (the default) encodes
        it if any pixel isn't opaque; **drop** ignores it, encoding the colour
        values as if every pixel were opaque, and never writes an alpha
        channel.

    **-background** *rgb*
    :   Blend *pmap* over the colour *rgb* (an integer **0xRRGGBB**) and encode
        the opaque result, with no alpha channel.  It can't be combined with
        **-alpha drop**.

    **-alpha_quality** *quality*
    :   The quality of the alpha channel, from 0 (smallest) to 100 (the
        default: lossless).  Below 100 libwebp reduces the alpha values to
        fewer levels before compressing them losslessly: 2 + *quality*/5 levels
        at 70 and below, 16 + (*quality* - 70) * 8 above it.  Values from 70
        down save the most: between 71 and 99 so many levels remain that the
        alpha channel can come out larger than at 100.

    **-alpha_filtering** *filter*
    :   The predictive filter applied to the alpha channel before compressing
        it: **none**, **fast** (the default) or **best** (much slower, for
        little gain).

    **-exact**
    :   Keep the colour values of fully transparent pixels.  By default they are
        discarded to compress better, which is right unless something will
        later remove the alpha channel and needs the hidden colours.

    **-use_sharp_yuv**
    :   Use the slower, more accurate RGB to YUV conversion, which keeps fine
        edges between saturated colours sharper.  Lossy only.

    **-thread_level** *level*
    :   Use threads while encoding: 0 (the default) uses none.  libwebp
        @LIBWEBP_VERSION@ accepts 0 or 1, and at 1 encodes the alpha channel in
        parallel with the colour data and splits up some of the analysis.  It
        only helps where more than one CPU is available.

    **-low_memory**
    :   Reduce the memory used by lossy encoding, at the cost of more CPU.

    **-target_size** *bytes*
    :   Search for the quality that gives an image of about *bytes* bytes,
        encoding the colour data up to **-pass** times: with the default of one
        pass there is no search to speak of, so give **-pass** too.  0 (the
        default) turns it off.  The alpha channel isn't included in the
        search.

    **-target_psnr** *dB*
    :   Like **-target_size**, but searching for a PSNR of *dB*.  0 (the
        default) turns it off.

    **-pass** *passes*
    :   The number of entropy-analysis passes, from 1 (the default) to 10:
        the most that **-target_size** and **-target_psnr** take to approach
        their target.

    **-stats** *varname*
    :   Set the variable *varname* to a dictionary of the statistics the
        encoder collected (**WebPAuxStats**), described under **STATISTICS**.

    The flags (**-lossless**, **-exact**, **-use_sharp_yuv**, **-low_memory**)
    take no value: present turns them on.

**pixel::webp::decode** ?*-option value* ...? *webp_data*
:   Decode the WebP image in the byte array *webp_data* and return it as a
    pmap.  A lossy or lossless image, with or without alpha, is supported; an
    animated one isn't.  The options, which must come before *webp_data*, are
    the libwebp decoder's (**WebPDecoderOptions**):

    **-scale** *width* *height*
    :   Decode to *width* by *height* pixels, scaling as part of decoding,
        which is much cheaper than decoding at full size and then scaling.  A
        0 dimension is derived from the other, keeping the aspect ratio
        (rounding up), so **-scale 300 0** gives an image 300 pixels wide.
        Each dimension can be up to 16383, and the image can be enlarged.
        libwebp's scaler is a simple one: for the best quality, decode at full
        size and scale with **pixel::scale_pmapf_lanczos**.

    **-use_threads**
    :   Decode using threads where libwebp can (the lossy filtering).  It only
        helps where more than one CPU is available.

    **-dithering_strength** *strength*
    :   Dither the colour of a lossy image, from 0 (the default, off) to 100,
        to hide the banding of strong compression.

    **-alpha_dithering_strength** *strength*
    :   Smooth an alpha channel that was reduced to fewer levels (encoded with
        an **-alpha_quality** below 100), from 0 (the default, off) to 100,
        hiding the steps between levels.  libwebp leaves an alpha channel with
        only 2 levels (**-alpha_quality** below 5) as it is.

**pixel::webp::info** *webp_data*
:   Return a dictionary of what the header of the WebP image in *webp_data*
    says, without decoding the image: **width** and **height** in pixels,
    **has_alpha** and **has_animation** (booleans), and **format**: **lossy**,
    **lossless** or **mixed** (an animation whose frames are of both kinds).
    The start of the image is enough.


# STATISTICS

The dictionary set by **-stats** has these keys:

**coded_size**
:   The size of the WebP image, in bytes.

**alpha_data_size**
:   The bytes of it spent on the alpha channel: 0 when no alpha channel was
    written.

**psnr**
:   A dictionary of the peak signal-to-noise ratio, in dB, of the **y**, **u**
    and **v** planes, **all** of them, and the **alpha** channel, measured
    against the encoder's input (after its RGB to YUV conversion).  99 means no
    difference.

**block_count**, **header_bytes**, **segment_size**, **segment_quant**, **segment_level**
:   Statistics of a lossy image's colour data: the counts of intra4, intra16
    and skipped macroblocks; the bytes spent on the header and on mode
    partition 0; and the number of macroblocks, quantizer and filter strength
    of each of the 4 segments.

**lossless_features**, **histogram_bits**, **transform_bits**, **cross_color_transform_bits**, **cache_bits**, **palette_size**, **lossless_size**, **lossless_hdr_size**, **lossless_data_size**
:   Statistics of the lossless encoder: of the whole image with
    **-lossless**, otherwise of the alpha channel.  **lossless_features** is a
    bitmask of the transforms used: 1 predictor, 2 cross-colour, 4 subtract
    green, 8 colour indexing.


# ERRORS

Errors in the arguments follow **parse_args**: an unknown option, a missing
argument or a value that isn't one of an option's choices have its usual
messages and error codes.  The others are:

**PIXEL WEBP RANGE** *name*
:   A numeric argument or option was outside its range.

**PIXEL WEBP ENCODE** *code*
:   libwebp rejected the configuration or failed to encode the image.  *code*
    is the name of libwebp's **WebPEncodingError**, without its **VP8_ENC_ERROR_**
    prefix (for example **INVALID_CONFIGURATION** or **OUT_OF_MEMORY**).

**PIXEL WEBP DECODE** *code*
:   *webp_data* couldn't be decoded, or for **pixel::webp::info**, its header
    couldn't be read.  *code* is **BITSTREAM_ERROR** for data
    that isn't WebP or is corrupt, **NOT_ENOUGH_DATA** for a truncated
    image, **ANIMATION** for an animated one, or another of libwebp's
    **VP8StatusCode** names without its **VP8_STATUS_** prefix.


# EXAMPLES

Encode as libwebp's simple API does:

~~~tcl
set webp [pixel::webp::encode $pmap 90]
~~~

Encode an image with soft transparency faster and smaller, by reducing the
alpha channel to 16 levels and encoding it in parallel with the colour data:

~~~tcl
set webp [pixel::webp::encode -alpha_quality 70 -thread_level 1 $pmap 90]
~~~

Flatten an image onto white, and find out how big each part of the result is:

~~~tcl
set webp [pixel::webp::encode -background 0xffffff -stats stats $pmap 85]
puts "[dict get $stats coded_size] bytes, PSNR [dict get $stats psnr all] dB"
~~~

Encode losslessly, and check that the pixels survive:

~~~tcl
set copy [pixel::webp::decode [pixel::webp::encode -lossless $pmap 75]]
~~~

Make a thumbnail 200 pixels wide, after checking the image's size:

~~~tcl
if {[dict get [pixel::webp::info $webp] width] > 200} {
    set thumb [pixel::webp::decode -scale 200 0 -use_threads $webp]
}
~~~


